    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Shadeland got its name when early settlers compared the wooded land to their eastern home, calling it Shadeland.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://shadeland.in.gov/about/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Bennett Taylor built a grain elevator in 1901, later known as Taylor Station, on the Louisville, New Albany, and Chicago Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://shadeland.in.gov/about/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town became famous for its cattle, and Taylor Station remains a local landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://shadeland.in.gov/about/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1827, settlers founded the Society of Friends and erected Shadeland’s first church.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
